WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.580
An axon grows toward the midline of the nervous system.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.480
It crosses. Then it treats that same midline as forbidden ground.

3
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.440
What changed?

4
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:16.263
Your left and right sides have to talk.

5
00:00:17.143 --> 00:00:21.583
Much touch and pain information from one side ends up in the opposite half of the

6
00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:22.583
brain.

7
00:00:22.883 --> 00:00:26.303
The two halves of the spinal cord also coordinate walking.

8
00:00:27.263 --> 00:00:31.043
Both jobs need commissural axons, axons that cross the midline.

9
00:00:32.016 --> 00:00:36.276
Their neighbours, ipsilateral axons, stay on the side where they started.

10
00:00:37.116 --> 00:00:38.196
They must never cross.

11
00:00:39.266 --> 00:00:41.426
A commissural axon has three jobs.

12
00:00:42.386 --> 00:00:43.386
Grow toward the midline.

13
00:00:44.146 --> 00:00:45.526
Enter it and leave it.

14
00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:47.046
And never come back.

15
00:00:47.966 --> 00:00:49.986
Each job fails in a different way.

16
00:00:50.746 --> 00:00:54.466
If the pull still beats the push after crossing, the axon stalls there.

17
00:00:55.416 --> 00:00:58.436
Get repelled too early, and it never crosses at all.

18
00:00:59.256 --> 00:01:02.236
Lose the repulsion after crossing, and it might wander back.

19
00:01:03.199 --> 00:01:04.779
Now look at the midline itself.

20
00:01:05.739 --> 00:01:07.499
In the fly it is a row of glial cells.

21
00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:10.419
In the spinal cord it is the floor plate.

22
00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:16.219
The same cells present the same molecules before, during and after an axon passes.

23
00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:18.399
The midline did not change.

24
00:01:19.399 --> 00:01:21.379
So the change has to be in the axon.

25
00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:23.679
That leaves three puzzles.

26
00:01:24.779 --> 00:01:27.099
Why does an axon drawn to the midline leave it?

27
00:01:27.839 --> 00:01:28.979
Why does it never come back?

28
00:01:29.819 --> 00:01:32.559
And why do its ipsilateral neighbours never cross at all?

29
00:01:33.499 --> 00:01:35.679
Here is the one idea to carry through.

30
00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:39.279
While an axon crosses, the midline stays the same.

31
00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.899
The axon changes what it can hear.

32
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:45.103
The midline makes a repellent called Slit.

33
00:01:45.943 --> 00:01:48.683
The growth cone carries Robo receptors that hear it.

34
00:01:49.566 --> 00:01:55.146
In a simple toy model, the axon's push is Slit times the repelling Robo activity on

35
00:01:55.146 --> 00:01:56.146
its growth cone.

36
00:01:56.866 --> 00:02:00.546
The axon is turned away only when that push passes a threshold.

37
00:02:01.616 --> 00:02:04.876
Ipsilateral axons keep the push high from the start.

38
00:02:05.816 --> 00:02:08.436
Commissural axons hold it low until they have crossed.

39
00:02:09.376 --> 00:02:10.376
Then they let it rise.

40
00:02:11.249 --> 00:02:13.449
Flies do this with a protein called Comm.

41
00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:17.389
Mammals do it with a relative of Robo, called Robo3.

42
00:02:18.432 --> 00:02:21.732
So a Slit and Robo mutant can be read with three questions.

43
00:02:22.772 --> 00:02:25.432
Is Slit there? Can the axon hear it?

44
00:02:26.172 --> 00:02:27.332
When does it start hearing it?

45
00:02:28.216 --> 00:02:33.256
The takeaway: the midline stays the same, and the axon changes what it can hear, and

46
00:02:33.256 --> 00:02:36.216
when. That is the repulsion side of the story.
